.TH QUANUXCTL-CRUCIBLE 1 "22 Feb 2026" "QuanuX 0.1.0" "QuanuX User Manual"
.SH NAME
quanuxctl\-crucible \- manage and orchestrate the isolated QuanuX Crucible backtesting engine.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B quanuxctl crucible
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B quanuxctl crucible
is the primary interface for initiating, monitoring, and aggressively querying the C++ QuanuX Crucible execution simulation. Crucible enforces process isolation, moving execution off the terminal shell via
.I os.setsid()
and trapping the runtime backtest logic inside an isolated OS process.
.PP
The backtester is powered by DuckDB Appenders bound to memory via Cython, granting it native throughput 100x faster than traditional Pandas/SQL engines.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start <strategy> [--version VERSION]
Ignites the simulator. It searches for a Foundry-generated test harness inside \fBserver/backtests/<strategy>_v<version>\fR.
If found, it forks the process and writes the PID to \fB/tmp/quanux_crucible.pid\fR.
.TP
.B stop
Sends a POSIX TERM signal to the engine PID. It waits 5 seconds before resorting to an aggressive SIGKILL to halt runway algorithms.
.TP
.B status
Reads the tracked PID and invokes psutil to return real-time CPU matching load (%) and RAM consumption inside the C++ 128-byte aligned memory pools.
.TP
.B report <strategy> [--version VERSION]
Instantaneous Telemetry Extraction. Bypasses the active Python context, loads the compiled Cython bindings from \fBQuanuX-Backtesting-Engine/python\fR, and executes the native \fBDuckDBFeeder::get_metrics_json()\fR query via the C++ DuckDB API.
.SH FILES
.I /tmp/quanux_crucible.pid
.RS
Stores the OS process ID for the currently running simulation engine.
.RE
.I server/backtests/<strategy_name>_v<version>/crucible.duckdb
.RS
The physical DuckDB state file written natively by the C++ engine.
.RE
.SH ARCHITECTURE NOTES
Crucible is uncompromising on performance. The \fBCrucibleTrade\fR struct aligns explicitly to 128 bytes to maintain memory locality inside CPU L1/L2 data caches. Modifying this explicitly padded struct without propagating changes down to the C++ \fIquanux_crucible.pxd\fR headers will result in fatal Segmentation Faults.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR quanuxctl (1),
.BR quanuxctl\-foundry (1)
.SH BUGS
No known bugs at present.
